nodecode-field-notes is a rewrite of itpartypattaya's itpartypattaya/hermes-field-notes:

  https://github.com/itpartypattaya/hermes-field-notes
  commit 74b566dd49ce6a9f249b7d1e5e12d25895592d42
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Anton Vaskov (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the skill `hermes-field-notes`: its note format (a
Markdown note with a flat frontmatter block of schema_version, id, title,
date, type, area, status and summary, and the rest in the body), its three
note kinds, its statuses, its patch notes with a separate checks file, and
its rule that a patch is checked read-only by static signs in the code tree --
contains, not_contains and upstream, one edition per range of versions, the
first edition whose bounds hold. What it answered about a check is what this
answers: OK when every sign is there, MISSING when a target is there and its
signs are gone, UPSTREAMED when the signs are gone and upstream's fix is in
place, UNKNOWN when the check could not decide (no edition matches, the
version is unknown, a target is gone, or the checks file does not decode).
Its INDEX.md and its doctor's one verdict line, its refusal to duplicate a
note id, and its four steps of procedure -- search before debugging, record
after a non-trivial diagnosis, update rather than duplicate, re-check after
an update -- are carried over as the tools' descriptions and the primer.

What was rewritten, and how: the original is a Python script behind a Hermes
skill and a set of slash commands. This is Common Lisp against the Nodecode
cell contract: the four commands the model used (search, new, patches check,
doctor) are four tools (fieldnote_search, fieldnote_new, fieldnote_check,
fieldnote_doctor) with JSON answers, the command line is the /fieldnotes
slash command, the store is the folder the section names, and the Hermes
version the checks compare against is the code tree's own VERSION file. The
notes are the same Markdown files with the same frontmatter, so a store from
the original reads here and back.

What was left out, and why: the bundled prose that is Hermes-specific --
the known-pitfalls list, the patch recipes, the patch-script and upstreaming
guides -- because they are about the Hermes core and its releases, not about
the store this cell keeps. The Telegram dashboard (one pinned message edited
in place) and its publish step are left out because a Nodecode cell posts
through its own channel cells; the hourly watch and its cron job are left out
because the cell is consulted by the model and by /fieldnotes instead of a
scheduler. `drift` (git changes since a note was verified) and the
`upstream.check` GitHub lookups are left out to keep every answer local and
offline: a patch note's checks read the tree on disk and nothing else.
`lint`'s secret-masking scan and `migrate` from older formats are left out
with them. LICENSE is the original repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
